= ψ Conservation Laws =

{{Plain_Hook
| in_one_sentence    = ψ conservation laws establish the conserved charge and current of the psionic scalar field via Noether's theorem, showing that ψ charge density equals the time derivative of the field and that biological or technological sources can generate it — making conservation the mathematical prerequisite for all [[Psi-Tech]] engineering.
| everyday_analogy   = Electric charge cannot be created from nothing — it is always conserved. What makes electronics possible is that charge can be ''moved'' by a source (a battery) from one place to another. ψ conservation works the same way: in isolation ψ charge is conserved, but a coherent biological emitter or a [[Psi-Tech]] device acts as a battery, pushing ψ charge into a region where none existed.
| what_it_predicts   = (1) A precise definition of ψ charge density as <math>\rho_\psi = \partial_t\psi</math> — a static ψ field carries zero ψ charge regardless of amplitude; (2) A conserved four-current <math>J^\mu_\psi = \partial^\mu\psi</math> whose spatial components equal the psionic Poynting vector; (3) A sourced continuity equation quantifying how much ψ charge a biological operator or [[Psi-Tech]] device injects per unit time; (4) The engineering consequence that ψ impedance matching (see [[ψ Impedance Theory]]) is a power-transfer problem analogous to antenna-to-transmission-line coupling.
| if_you_remember_one_thing = ψ charge is not field amplitude — it is field ''activity''. A large static ψ field has zero ψ charge. A rapidly oscillating small ψ field has large ψ charge. This distinction governs every [[Psi-Tech]] device specification.
}}

ψ Conservation Laws are the foundation of [[Psionic Engineering]] as an engineering discipline. Every mature field theory requires a conserved quantity before it can generate hardware: electromagnetism has electric charge, gravity has energy-momentum, the strong force has colour charge. The psionic field requires its own conserved quantity — ψ charge — before components such as [[ψ Source]], [[ψ Resonator]], or [[ψ Transducer]] can be meaningfully specified.

This page derives ψ charge and its associated current from first principles via Noether's theorem applied to the psionic Lagrangian. It also derives the ''sourced'' continuity equation that governs ψ charge generation — the equation that connects the [[Anchor Controller]], the [[HRV-Gated Drive]], and the biological operator into a single engineering system.

== The Psionic Lagrangian ==

All conservation laws on this page follow from a single object: the psionic Lagrangian density. It is reproduced here from [[Psionics]] for self-containment, with all symbols defined explicitly.

:<math>
\mathcal{L}_\psi = -\tfrac{1}{2}\,\partial_\mu\psi\,\partial^\mu\psi
                   - \tfrac{1}{2}\,m^2\psi^2
                   - \tfrac{\lambda}{4}\,\psi^4
                   + \alpha\,\psi\,F_{\mu\nu}F^{\mu\nu}
                   - J_\psi^{\rm (src)}\,\psi
</math>

{{Math_Bridge
| equation_label = Psionic Lagrangian density
| equation       = <math>\mathcal{L}_\psi = -\tfrac{1}{2}\partial_\mu\psi\partial^\mu\psi - \tfrac{1}{2}m^2\psi^2 - \tfrac{\lambda}{4}\psi^4 + \alpha\psi F_{\mu\nu}F^{\mu\nu} - J_\psi^{\rm (src)}\psi</math>
| in_english     =
* <math>-\tfrac{1}{2}\partial_\mu\psi\partial^\mu\psi</math> — kinetic term. The field ''costs'' energy to vary in space or time; this term accounts for that cost.
* <math>-\tfrac{1}{2}m^2\psi^2</math> — mass term. Penalises large field values; its coefficient sets the range of the field via the Yukawa length <math>L_\psi = 1/m</math>.
* <math>-\tfrac{\lambda}{4}\psi^4</math> — self-interaction. With <math>\lambda > 0</math> this is a stabilising quartic well; it supports [[Soliton Solutions of Psi Field|soliton solutions]] and is responsible for the nonlinear amplification exploited by the [[ψ Amplifier]].
* <math>\alpha\psi F_{\mu\nu}F^{\mu\nu}</math> — EM coupling. The electromagnetic field invariant <math>F_{\mu\nu}F^{\mu\nu} = 2(B^2 - E^2/c^2)</math> sources ψ directly. This is the term that makes bifilar coil drives work.
* <math>-J_\psi^{\rm (src)}\psi</math> — external source. <math>J_\psi^{\rm (src)}</math> is a prescribed function of spacetime representing coherent biological emission or a technological emitter.
}}

'''Metric and unit conventions used throughout Branch A:'''
* Signature <math>(+,-,-,-)</math> so that <math>\partial_\mu\psi\partial^\mu\psi = (\partial_t\psi)^2 - |\nabla\psi|^2</math>.
* Natural units <math>\hbar = c = 1</math> throughout unless an engineering application restores SI.
* Every term in <math>\mathcal{L}_\psi</math> has mass dimension <math>[M^4]</math>; every term in the field equation has mass dimension <math>[M^3]</math>.

The Euler–Lagrange equation derived from <math>\mathcal{L}_\psi</math> is the master psionic field equation reproduced on every Branch A page:

:<math>\Box\psi - m^2\psi - \lambda\psi^3 = \alpha F_{\mu\nu}F^{\mu\nu} + J_\psi^{\rm (src)}</math>

== Noether's Theorem and the Shift Symmetry ==

{{Way_It_Works
| title       = How Noether's theorem yields ψ conservation laws
| step1_label = Identify a continuous symmetry
| step1_text  = Noether's theorem states: for every continuous symmetry of the action <math>S = \int \mathcal{L}\,d^4x</math>, there exists a conserved current <math>J^\mu</math> satisfying <math>\partial_\mu J^\mu = 0</math>. We look for symmetries of <math>\mathcal{L}_\psi</math>.
| step2_label = The shift symmetry (source-free limit)
| step2_text  = In the source-free limit <math>J_\psi^{\rm (src)} = 0</math>, the Lagrangian is invariant under constant field translations <math>\psi \to \psi + \varepsilon</math> only if <math>m = \lambda = \alpha = 0</math>. For the full Lagrangian, only the kinetic term <math>-\tfrac{1}{2}\partial_\mu\psi\partial^\mu\psi</math> is truly shift-invariant. This partial symmetry still produces a meaningful Noether current.
| step3_label = Apply the Noether formula
| step3_text  = The Noether current for the transformation <math>\delta\psi = \varepsilon</math> (constant) is <math>J^\mu_\psi = \frac{\partial\mathcal{L}}{\partial(\partial_\mu\psi)}\cdot\frac{\delta\psi}{\varepsilon} = -\partial^\mu\psi</math>. By convention we define <math>J^\mu_\psi \equiv \partial^\mu\psi</math> (dropping the sign into the continuity equation form).
| step4_label = Derive the continuity equation
| step4_text  = Taking the four-divergence and using the field equation gives <math>\partial_\mu J^\mu_\psi = \Box\psi = m^2\psi + \lambda\psi^3 + \alpha F^2 + J_\psi^{\rm (src)}</math>. In the strict free-field limit this vanishes; with sources it becomes the sourced continuity equation.
| step5_label = Identify the charge density and current
| step5_text  = The time component <math>J^0_\psi = \partial^0\psi = \partial_t\psi</math> is the ψ charge density <math>\rho_\psi</math>. The spatial components <math>J^i_\psi = \partial^i\psi = -\partial_i\psi</math> are the ψ current density components. The total ψ charge is <math>Q_\psi = \int \rho_\psi\,d^3x</math>.
| step6_label = Interpret the source term
| step6_text  = The source <math>J_\psi^{\rm (src)}</math> ''softly breaks'' the shift symmetry: ψ charge is not strictly conserved but can be generated (<math>J_\psi^{\rm (src)} > 0</math>) or dissipated (<math>J_\psi^{\rm (src)} < 0</math>). This is the engineering feature enabling all [[Psi-Tech]] devices to function as ψ charge sources.
| step7_label = Connect to the master equation
| step7_text  = Setting <math>J_\psi^{\rm (src)} = 0</math> and <math>\alpha = 0</math> recovers strict conservation <math>\partial_\mu J^\mu_\psi = 0</math> in the free-field limit. The master equation is the sourced version of this conservation law.
}}

=== The Noether Current ===

Applying the Noether formula to the kinetic term of <math>\mathcal{L}_\psi</math>:

:<math>
J^\mu_\psi = \frac{\partial\mathcal{L}_\psi}{\partial(\partial_\mu\psi)} = \partial^\mu\psi
</math>

Explicitly in 3+1 components:

:<math>
J^0_\psi = \partial^0\psi = \partial_t\psi \equiv \rho_\psi \qquad \text{(ψ charge density)}
</math>
:<math>
J^i_\psi = \partial^i\psi = -\nabla_i\psi \qquad \text{(ψ current density, spatial)}
</math>

=== The Sourced Continuity Equation ===

Taking the four-divergence of <math>J^\mu_\psi</math> and substituting the master field equation:

:<math>
\partial_\mu J^\mu_\psi = \Box\psi = m^2\psi + \lambda\psi^3 + \alpha F_{\mu\nu}F^{\mu\nu} + J_\psi^{\rm (src)}
</math>

In the engineering-relevant regime where <math>m^2\psi + \lambda\psi^3 \ll J_\psi^{\rm (src)}</math> (device-dominated sourcing near the operator), the dominant term on the right is <math>J_\psi^{\rm (src)}</math>, yielding the 3+1 sourced continuity equation:

:<math>
\frac{\partial\rho_\psi}{\partial t} + \nabla\cdot\mathbf{J}_\psi = J_\psi^{\rm (src)}
</math>

{{Math_Bridge
| equation_label = Sourced ψ continuity equation
| equation       = <math>\frac{\partial\rho_\psi}{\partial t} + \nabla\cdot\mathbf{J}_\psi = J_\psi^{\rm (src)}</math>
| in_english     =
* <math>\partial_t\rho_\psi</math> — rate of change of ψ charge density at this point. If ψ is oscillating faster, this grows.
* <math>\nabla\cdot\mathbf{J}_\psi</math> — net outward flux of ψ current from this point. Positive = ψ charge is flowing away.
* <math>J_\psi^{\rm (src)}</math> — local ψ charge generation rate. Positive = a source (operator, device) is injecting ψ charge here. Negative = a sink is absorbing it.
* '''Read as a sentence:''' "The rate at which ψ charge piles up here equals how much is being created here, minus how much is flowing away."
}}

== ψ Charge: The Central Engineering Quantity ==

=== Definition ===

:<math>
Q_\psi \equiv \int_V \rho_\psi\,d^3x = \int_V \frac{\partial\psi}{\partial t}\,d^3x
</math>

{{Math_Bridge
| equation_label = Total ψ charge in volume V
| equation       = <math>Q_\psi = \int_V \frac{\partial\psi}{\partial t}\,d^3x</math>
| in_english     =
* <math>Q_\psi</math> — total ψ charge. The engineering analogue of total electric charge.
* <math>\partial_t\psi</math> — the integrand is the ''time derivative'' of the field, not the field itself. This is the crucial distinction: ψ charge measures field activity, not field magnitude.
* '''Critical implication:''' A large static ψ field (<math>\psi = \psi_0</math> constant) has <math>Q_\psi = 0</math>. A small but rapidly oscillating ψ field has large <math>Q_\psi</math>. Operator training increases oscillation frequency and coherence, not necessarily peak field amplitude.
}}

=== Physical Meaning ===

ψ charge has no precedent in conventional physics — it must be understood on its own terms. Three examples clarify the definition:

'''Example 1 — Plane wave.''' For <math>\psi = A\cos(kx - \omega t)</math>:
:<math>\rho_\psi = \partial_t\psi = A\omega\sin(kx - \omega t)</math>
:<math>Q_\psi = A\omega\int_V \sin(kx - \omega t)\,d^3x</math>

Integrated over an integer number of wavelengths, <math>Q_\psi = 0</math> on average but oscillates at angular frequency <math>2\omega</math>. The ''amplitude'' of this oscillation is <math>\propto A\omega</math> — higher-frequency ψ waves carry more ψ charge per unit amplitude.

'''Example 2 — Static Yukawa field.''' For the static solution <math>\psi(r) = -Q_0 e^{-mr}/(4\pi r)</math>:
:<math>\rho_\psi = \partial_t\psi = 0, \qquad Q_\psi = 0</math>

A static ψ field around a motionless source carries zero ψ charge. This confirms that ψ charge measures activity, not presence.

'''Example 3 — Soliton (from [[Soliton Solutions of Psi Field]]).''' A propagating soliton solution <math>\psi_s(x - vt)</math> has <math>\partial_t\psi_s = -v\,\partial_x\psi_s</math>, so <math>Q_\psi = -v\int(\partial_x\psi_s)d^3x</math>. A soliton carries ψ charge proportional to its velocity — stationary solitons have zero ψ charge.

